		 	<div class="component component-content-item component-contributor-notes null">
		        
    	
                

    <div class="content-box box no-border no-padding no-header vertical-margin-bottom null">
        
        <div class="content-box-body ">
            
                    
        
        <h2 class="text-headline"></h2>
        
            <div class="contributor-note"><contributor-notes encoding-type="nlm"><div id="n101">
<p>© 2026 American Meteorological Society. This published article is licensed under the terms of the default AMS reuse license. For information regarding reuse of this content and general copyright information, consult the AMS Copyright Policy (<a href="http://www.ametsoc.org/PUBSReuseLicenses" target="_blank">www.ametsoc.org/PUBSReuseLicenses</a>).</p></div><div class="corresp"><corresp xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" id="cor1"><i>Corresponding author</i>: Seok-Woo Son, <a href="mailto:seokwooson@snu.ac.kr">seokwooson@snu.ac.kr</a></corresp></div></contributor-notes></div>
        
    
    
                
        </div>
    </div>


            
    
		    </div>
		 

    

    

    
	


        
                
        
        
        
        
		
        
    
                

        


    
                                    
                                
	        
	        	
	        </div>
	    
	    
	    
	    
	    
	    
	    

		
    


	     
    
        
    
                    
    </div>

    
    


                

        


    
                                    
                                
                                    
                                    
        
            
                    

    <div id="container-62949-item-62890" class="container-item">
        
                        
        
            
                    
            

    

    

	
		 	<div class="component component-content-item component-content-html ">
		        
    	
                

    <div class="content-box box no-border  no-header vertical-margin-bottom null">
        
        <div class="content-box-body ">
            
                    
        
		<div data-datatype="fthtml" data-docuri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" class="counterData"></div>
		<div class="bodyCssRoot" id="contentRoot"><div id="articleBody"><div class="keywords mb-3"><span id="keywordsLabel">Keywords: </span><a href="/search?q=Extratropical+transition">Extratropical transition</a>; <a href="/search?q=Tropical+cyclones">Tropical cyclones</a>; <a href="/search?q=Kinetic+energy">Kinetic energy</a></div>
<div class="section border-bottom border-bottom-solid border-bottom-medium" id="s1">
<h2 id="d9691133e197">1. Introduction</h2>
<p>Tropical cyclones (TCs) that travel to the midlatitudes often undergo profound structural changes, transitioning into extratropical cyclones (ETCs). This transition, known as the extratropical transition (ET), evolves as the storm interacts with the midlatitude baroclinic environment (e.g., <a id="ref_bib34" href="#bib34">Jones et al. 2003</a>; <a id="ref_bib18" href="#bib18">Evans et al. 2017</a>). The ET is often associated with high-impact hazards, such as strong wind expansion (<a id="ref_bib51" href="#bib51">Powell 1982</a>; <a id="ref_bib17" href="#bib17">Evans and Hart 2008</a>), large ocean waves (<a id="ref_bib8" href="#bib8">Bowyer and MacAfee 2005</a>), and heavy rainfall (<a id="ref_bib5" href="#bib5">Atallah and Bosart 2003</a>; <a id="ref_bib13" href="#bib13">Chen 2011</a>), allowing the storm to maintain or even expand its hazardous footprint well beyond the TC phase.</p>
<p>Previous studies have documented ET processes in various basins, including the western North Pacific (e.g., <a id="ref_bib39" href="#bib39">Klein et al. 2000</a>; <a id="ref_bib38" href="#bib38">Kitabatake 2011</a>), eastern Pacific (<a id="ref_bib66" href="#bib66">Wood and Ritchie 2014</a>), North Atlantic (e.g., <a id="ref_bib7" href="#bib7">Bosart and Lackmann 1995</a>; <a id="ref_bib31" href="#bib31">Hart and Evans 2001</a>), and Southern Hemisphere (e.g., <a id="ref_bib21" href="#bib21">Foley and Hanstrum 1994</a>; <a id="ref_bib62" href="#bib62">Sinclair 2002</a>). Although regional differences exist, transitioning TCs exhibit a similar structural evolution. As a TC moves into the midlatitudes, it encounters decreasing sea surface temperature and increasing vertical wind shear. This leads to a weakening of its tropical characteristics, such as deep, moist, and warm-core structures. Simultaneously, the storm develops pronounced asymmetric structures, including dry intrusions, poleward-expanding cirrus shields, and frontal cloud bands (e.g., <a id="d7974185e1143" href="#bib39">Klein et al. 2000</a>; <a id="ref_bib29" href="#bib29">Harr and Elsberry 2000</a>; <a id="ref_bib57" href="#bib57">Ritchie and Elsberry 2001</a>). Consistent with these changes, the precipitation and wind fields show asymmetric evolutions (<a id="ref_bib1" href="#bib1">Abraham et al. 2004</a>; <a id="ref_bib6" href="#bib6">Atallah et al. 2007</a>; <a id="ref_bib24" href="#bib24">Fujibe and Kitabatake 2007</a>; <a id="d7974185e1162" href="#bib17">Evans and Hart 2008</a>). As the storm becomes fully embedded within the midlatitude baroclinic zone, it transitions to a distinct cold-cored extratropical system and often exhibits well-defined warm and cold sectors with pronounced frontal boundaries.</p>
<p>The midlatitude environment both drives and responds to the transitioning TCs (<a id="d7974185e1168" href="#bib18">Evans et al. 2017</a>; <a id="ref_bib37" href="#bib37">Keller et al. 2019</a>). Throughout the evolution, upper-level divergent outflow of the TC is crucial, being primarily driven by diabatic heating but partially influenced by dry dynamic forcing (<a id="ref_bib26" href="#bib26">Grams and Archambault 2016</a>; <a id="ref_bib52" href="#bib52">Quinting and Jones 2016</a>). The divergent TC outflow modifies the background flow by advecting low potential vorticity (PV) air from the subtropics into the midlatitudes, lifting the tropopause and amplifying the downstream ridge (<a id="d7974185e1180" href="#bib7">Bosart and Lackmann 1995</a>; <a id="ref_bib3" href="#bib3">Archambault et al. 2013</a>; <a id="ref_bib27" href="#bib27">Grams et al. 2013a</a>). The enhanced PV gradient along the tropopause often anchors and intensifies the upper-level jet, while the upper-level divergent and anticyclonic PV anomaly inhibits eastward propagation of the upstream trough and wraps it up cyclonically (<a id="ref_bib2" href="#bib2">Agustí-Panareda et al. 2004</a>; <a id="ref_bib56" href="#bib56">Riemer et al. 2008</a>; <a id="d7974185e1196" href="#bib27">Grams et al. 2013a</a>; <a id="d7974185e1199" href="#bib26">Grams and Archambault 2016</a>). When the upstream trough and the transitioning TC are optimally phased, it can promote reintensification of a cyclone and downstream development (<a id="ref_bib58" href="#bib58">Ritchie and Elsberry 2003</a>, <a id="ref_bib59" href="#bib59">2007</a>; <a id="ref_bib28" href="#bib28">Grams et al. 2013b</a>; <a id="ref_bib4" href="#bib4">Archambault et al. 2015</a>; <a id="d7974185e1215" href="#bib26">Grams and Archambault 2016</a>; <a id="ref_bib53" href="#bib53">Riboldi et al. 2019</a>).</p>
<p>To objectively monitor the transformation of TCs into extratropical systems, the cyclone phase space (CPS) framework has been employed in both research and operations (<a id="ref_bib19" href="#bib19">Evans and Hart 2003</a>; <a id="ref_bib30" href="#bib30">Hart 2003</a>; <a id="d7974185e1230" href="#bib38">Kitabatake 2011</a>; <a id="d7974185e1233" href="#bib66">Wood and Ritchie 2014</a>). CPS characterizes the thermal and kinematic structure of a cyclone using parameters that represent the lower- and upper-tropospheric thermal wind and the storm asymmetry (<a id="d7974185e1236" href="#bib30">Hart 2003</a>). This framework allows the structural evolution of a TC to be interpreted in terms of its transition from a symmetric warm-core tropical system to an asymmetric cold-core extratropical system. Although CPS provides a continuous and quantitative depiction of structural evolution of a cyclone, understanding these structural changes requires a dynamical interpretation.</p>
<p>The present study adopts a local energetics framework to assess how TC–midlatitude interaction affects ET. <a id="ref_bib43" href="#bib43">Lorenz (1955)</a> laid the groundwork for atmospheric energetics by introducing available potential energy and developing a global energy cycle. By decomposing basic variables into zonal-mean and eddy components, this framework quantifies the generation, dissipation, and conversion of kinetic energy and available potential energy. This conceptual framework, known as the Lorenz energy cycle, has become the cornerstone for understanding the fundamental energetics of the atmospheric general circulation. Building on this, local energy budget approaches further characterize the regional energy structure and evolution. Local kinetic energy budgets have been used to examine the energy dispersion in baroclinic waves, establishing the concept of downstream development (<a id="ref_bib48" href="#bib48">Orlanski and Katzfey 1991</a>; <a id="ref_bib49" href="#bib49">Orlanski and Chang 1993</a>; <a id="ref_bib50" href="#bib50">Orlanski and Sheldon 1995</a>). Studies on locally available potential energy have linked regional energy reservoirs to storm-track activity and the thermodynamic potential for organized ascent such as warm conveyor belts (<a id="ref_bib47" href="#bib47">Novak and Tailleux 2018</a>; <a id="ref_bib25" href="#bib25">Gertler et al. 2023</a>).</p>
<p><a id="ref_bib44" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a> proposed a local version of the complete Lorenz energy cycle. By introducing interaction energy and boundary flux terms, this framework distinguishes energy conversions within the local domain from energy exchanges across its boundaries, providing a local energy cycle that encompasses both the kinetic and available potential energy of the mean and eddy fields. This framework has been applied to paleoclimate (<a id="ref_bib45" href="#bib45">Murakami et al. 2011</a>) and energy cascades in heavy rainfall events and their interactions with the large-scale background flow (<a id="ref_bib22" href="#bib22">Fu et al. 2016</a>, <a id="ref_bib23" href="#bib23">2018</a>), demonstrating its usefulness across various temporal and spatial scales. However, Murakami’s local energy cycle is formulated within an Eulerian framework, and its application to moving systems such as TCs requires reformulation into a cyclone-following framework.</p>
<p>In this study, local energetics during ET are examined and compared with those during the TC. To this end, the local energy cycle of <a id="d7974185e1279" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a> is modified for use in a cyclone frame of reference. The proposed framework is applied to TC Hagibis (2019), which made landfall in Japan and caused widespread damage. TC Hagibis exhibited pronounced structural changes through interactions with the baroclinic environment in midlatitudes, making it a representative ET case (<a id="ref_bib33" href="#bib33">Ito and Ichikawa 2021</a>; <a id="ref_bib67" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. 2022</a>; <a id="ref_bib46" href="#bib46">Nayak and Takemi 2023</a>).</p>
<p>The rest of this paper is organized as follows. <a id="ref_s2" href="#s2">Section 2</a> explains the dataset and methods, including the cyclone-following local eddy energetics. <a id="ref_s3" href="#s3">Section 3</a> presents an overview of TC Hagibis, including its CPS evolution and synoptic environment. <a id="ref_s4" href="#s4">Section 4</a> applies the local eddy energetics to ET of Hagibis. The results are summarized and discussed in <a id="ref_s5" href="#s5">section 5</a>.</p></div>
<div class="section border-bottom border-bottom-solid border-bottom-medium" id="s2">
<h2 id="d9691133e415">2. Data and methods</h2><div class="section" id="s2a">
<h3 id="d9691133e419">a. Dataset</h3>
<p>The best track data of the Regional Specialized Meteorological Center Tokyo-Typhoon Center, operated by the Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA), are used for TC identification. The dataset provides at least 6-hourly (0000, 0600, 1200, and 1800 UTC) observations of longitude, latitude, central pressure, maximum sustained wind speed, storm category (i.e., tropical depression, tropical storm, typhoon, or extratropical cyclone), and wind radii for 30 and 50 kt (1 kt ≈ 0.51 m s<sup>−1</sup>) winds at 10 m above ground level. Satellite images from the <i>Geostationary Korea Multi-Purpose Satellite-2A</i> (<i>GK2A</i>) are also used to examine the structural evolution of the TC. The background fields are analyzed with the Japanese Reanalysis of Three Quarters of a Century (JRA-3Q; <a id="ref_bib40" href="#bib40">Kosaka et al. 2024</a>) reanalysis data at 1.25° × 1.25° horizontal resolution, 45 pressure levels, and 6-hourly temporal resolution. Although this spatial resolution is too coarse to resolve the detailed TC structure, it is sufficient to explore the synoptic-scale features of TC and its interaction with the large-scale environment. Although not shown, the overall results are robust to the choice of reanalysis dataset (JRA-3Q vs ERA5) and spatial resolution (0.25° × 0.25° vs 1.5° × 1.5°).</p></div>
<div class="section" id="s2b">
<h3 id="d9691133e438">b. Cyclone phase space</h3>
<div class="p">The ET onset and completion are defined using the CPS diagram (<a id="d7974185e1340" href="#bib30">Hart 2003</a>; <a id="d7974185e1343" href="#bib19">Evans and Hart 2003</a>). The CPS employs three different parameters: the storm-relative geopotential thickness asymmetry between the right and left sides of the storm at 900–600 hPa (<i>B</i>), the low-level thermal wind between 900- and 600-hPa levels (<inline-formula id="IE1" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM1"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula>), and the upper-level thermal wind between 600- and 300-hPa levels (<inline-formula id="IE2" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM2"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula>). The thermal asymmetry of the storm <i>B</i> is calculated as<div id="E1" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M1"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mn>600</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mn>900</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mn>600</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mn>900</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(1)</label></div>where <i>Z</i> is the geopotential height, <i>R</i> and <i>L</i> denote the right and left sides of the storm relative to its direction of motion, and the overbar indicates the area mean within a 500-km radius of the storm center. The temperature and depth of the storm core are represented by lower-tropospheric thermal wind (<inline-formula id="IE3" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM3"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula>), which is calculated as<div id="E2" class="formula"><math id="M2" display="block"><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>Δ</mtext><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>ln</mtext><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>900</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>600</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo></math><label>(2)</label></div>and upper-tropospheric thermal wind (<inline-formula id="IE4" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM4"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula>), which is calculated as<div id="E3" class="formula"><math id="M3" display="block"><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>Δ</mtext><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>ln</mtext><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>600</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>300</mn><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msubsup><mo>.</mo></math><label>(3)</label></div></div>
<p>A positive <inline-formula id="IE5" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM5"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> indicates a warm-core system, while a negative <inline-formula id="IE6" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM6"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> indicates a cold-core system. Combined with <inline-formula id="IE7" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM7"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula>, <inline-formula id="IE8" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM8"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo> </mo></mrow></math></inline-formula> offers a measure of the vertical extent of the storm core. For example, positive <inline-formula id="IE9" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM9"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> and positive <inline-formula id="IE10" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM10"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> indicate a deep warm-core storm. In contrast, positive <inline-formula id="IE11" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM11"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo> </mo></mrow></math></inline-formula> but negative <inline-formula id="IE12" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM12"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> indicates a shallow warm-core storm.</p>
<p>The onset of ET is typically defined as the time when <i>B</i> exceeds 10 m, corresponding to a mature or developing frontal structure (<a id="d7974185e1597" href="#bib30">Hart 2003</a>; <a id="d7974185e1600" href="#bib19">Evans and Hart 2003</a>). The completion of ET is then defined as the time when <inline-formula id="IE13" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM13"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> becomes negative, indicating a transition to a cold-core system (<a id="d7974185e1612" href="#bib30">Hart 2003</a>; <a id="d7974185e1616" href="#bib19">Evans and Hart 2003</a>).</p></div>
<div class="section" id="s2c">
<h3 id="d9691133e725">c. Local eddy energetics</h3>
<div class="p">In this study, the transition from TC to ET is dynamically examined in the context of local energetics. The local energetics framework, which is used to characterize TC and ET, follows <a id="d7974185e1628" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a> but with modifications. The derivation begins with the primitive equations in the spherical pressure coordinate:<div id="E4a" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M4"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>υ</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mi>u</mi><mi>υ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>υ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(4a)</label></div><div id="E4b" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M5"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>υ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>υ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>υ</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>υ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>υ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(4b)</label></div><div id="E4c" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M6"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(4c)</label></div><div id="E4d" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M7"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>υ</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(4d)</label></div><div id="E4e" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M8"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>υ</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(4e)</label></div>where <i>λ</i>, <i>φ</i>, and <i>p</i> are the longitude, latitude, and pressure, respectively; <i>t</i> is the time; <i>u</i> and <i>υ</i> are the zonal and meridional winds, respectively; <i>ω</i> is the <i>p</i> velocity; Φ is the geopotential; <i>a</i> is the radius of Earth; <i>f</i> is the Coriolis parameter; <inline-formula id="IE14" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM14"><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></inline-formula> is the horizontal damping; <i>Q</i> is the diabatic heating; <i>p</i><sub>0</sub> is the reference pressure; <i>C<sub>p</sub></i> is the atmospheric specific heat at constant pressure; <i>κ</i> is the ratio of gas constant to the specific heat (=<i>R</i>/<i>C<sub>p</sub></i>); <i>θ</i> is the potential temperature; and <i>α</i> is the specific volume.</div>
<div class="p">By dividing the basic variables <i>u</i>, <i>υ</i>, <i>ω</i>, Φ, <i>θ</i>, and <i>α</i> into time-mean <inline-formula id="IE15" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM15"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>·</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></inline-formula> and transient eddy (·)′ components (e.g., <inline-formula id="IE16" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM16"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></math></inline-formula>) and taking only the eddy components, the equations for the eddy components in Eqs. <a id="ref_E5d" href="#E5d">(5a)–(5d)</a> are obtained as follows:<div id="E5a" class="formula"><math id="M9" display="block"><mtable columnalign="left"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">¯</mo></mover><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mtext>Φ</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math><label>(5a)</label></div><div id="E5b" class="formula"><math id="M10" display="block"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">¯</mo></mover><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mtext>Φ</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo></math><label>(5b)</label></div><div id="E5c" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M11"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup> <mtext>Φ</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(5c)</label></div><div id="E5d" class="formula"><math id="M12" display="block"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">¯</mo></mover><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><mfrac><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math><label>(5d)</label></div>where <strong>u</strong> = (<i>u</i>, <i>υ</i>, <i>ω</i>) is the three-dimensional wind vector and ∇ is the differential operator in spherical coordinates. Unlike in <a id="d7974185e2437" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a>, the time mean is defined here as a 30-day running mean, which is sufficiently long to represent the background flow while ensuring that perturbations are well separated from the mean. In this regard, the time-mean variable represents the low-frequency variability of background flow. Although not shown, the overall results remain unchanged when a 10- or 60-day running mean is applied.</div>
<div class="p">By applying the inner product to Eqs. <a id="ref_E5c" href="#E5c">(5a)–(5c)</a> with <strong>u</strong>′ = (<i>u</i>′, <i>υ</i>′, <i>ω</i>′), the eddy kinetic energy (EKE, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) equation is derived:<div id="E6" class="formula"><math id="M13" display="block"><mtable columnalign="left"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msup><mtext>Φ</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math><label>(6)</label></div>where<div id="E7" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M14"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math><label>(7)</label></div></div>
<div class="p">The second term on the left-hand side of Eq. <a id="ref_E6" href="#E6">(6)</a> represents the advection of EKE. To focus on EKE redistribution around the cyclone, this term is rearranged by considering the phase speed of the cyclone (<strong>u</strong><i><sub>c</sub></i>) and the adjusted background flow (<strong>u</strong><sub>adj</sub> = <strong>u</strong> − <strong>u</strong><i><sub>c</sub></i>) as in <a id="ref_bib60" href="#bib60">Rivière et al. (2015)</a>. The first two terms in Eq. <a id="d7974185e2693" href="#E6">(6)</a> are then written in the cyclone frame of reference (or the cyclone-following framework) as follows:<div id="E8a" class="formula"><math id="M15" display="block"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mtext>adj</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></math><label>(8a)</label></div>where<div id="E8b" class="formula"><math id="M16" display="block"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math><label>(8b)</label></div>Here, <i>D</i>/<i>Dt</i> is the time operator in the cyclone frame of reference, rather than the usual Lagrangian derivative (<a id="d7974185e2780" href="#bib60">Rivière et al. 2015</a>). The second term on the right-hand side of Eq. <a id="ref_E8a" href="#E8a">(8a)</a> represents relative advection with respect to TC movement. In this study, this term is referred to simply as the advection term. By assuming a nondivergent condition and neglecting higher-order perturbation terms, the EKE equation is obtained as<div id="E9" class="formula"><math id="M17" display="block"><mtable columnalign="left"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><msup><mtext>Φ</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mtext>adj</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math><label>(9)</label></div></div>
<p>This formulation is a rearrangement of the Eulerian equation of <a id="d7974185e2905" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a> in the cyclone frame of reference.</p>
<div class="p">By multiplying Eq. <a id="d7974185e2911" href="#E5d">(5d)</a> by <i>θ</i>′, the thermodynamic equation can be written as<div id="E10" class="formula"><math id="M18" display="block"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><msup><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><msup><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></math><label>(10)</label></div>where ⟨·⟩ denotes the global average. By multiplying Eq. <a id="ref_E10" href="#E10">(10)</a> by <i>C<sub>p</sub></i>(<i>p</i>/<i>p</i><sub>0</sub>)<sup>2</sup><i><sup>κ</sup>γ</i>, the equation is rewritten as<div id="E11" class="formula"><math id="M19" display="block"><mtable columnalign="left"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="true">[</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><msup><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math><label>(11)</label></div></div>
<div class="p">After neglecting higher-order perturbation terms and the contribution associated with the vertical gradient of γ, and rearranging the eddy available potential energy (EAPE; <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) advection (the second term on the left-hand side) by considering the cyclone phase speed and the adjusted background flow, the EAPE equation is derived:<div id="E12" class="formula"><math id="M20" display="block"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mtext>adj</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math><label>(12)</label></div></div>
<div class="p">The <i>A<sub>E</sub></i> is defined as<div id="E13" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M21"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(13)</label></div>where <i>γ</i> denotes the static stability index of the dry atmosphere, defined as<div id="E14" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M22"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(14)</label></div>and <i>T</i> is the temperature.</div>
<div class="p">From Eqs. <a id="ref_E9" href="#E9">(9)</a> and <a id="ref_E12" href="#E12">(12)</a>, one can express local eddy energy equations in the cyclone frame of reference as<div id="E15" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M23"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(15)</label></div><div id="E16" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M24"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math><label>(16)</label></div></div>
<div class="p">The right-hand side terms of Eq. <a id="d7974185e3625" href="#E15">(15)</a> are<div id="E17a" class="formula"><math id="M25" display="block"><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><msup><mtext>Φ</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></math><label>(17a)</label></div><div id="E17b" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M26"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(17b)</label></div><div id="E17c" class="formula"><math id="M27" display="block"><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></math><label>(17c)</label></div><div id="E17d" class="formula"><math id="M28" display="block"><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></math><label>(17d)</label></div><div id="E17e" class="formula"><math id="M29" display="block"><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mtext>adj</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math><label>(17e)</label></div></div>
<div class="p">Those of Eq. <a id="d7974185e3799" href="#E16">(16)</a> are<div id="E18a" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M30"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(18a)</label></div><div id="E18b" class="formula"><math id="M31" display="block"><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mo mathvariant="bold-italic">′</mo></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></math><label>(18b)</label></div><div id="E18c" class="formula"><math id="M32" display="block"><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">u</mi><mtext>adj</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math><label>(18c)</label></div>The symbols <i>B</i>(*), <i>D</i>(*), <i>A</i>(*), and <i>G</i>(*) denote the lateral boundary flux when spatially averaged over the storm area, dissipation by damping which is mostly due to surface friction, advection by background flow, and eddy energy generation, respectively. The symbol <i>C</i>(*1, *2) denotes the energy conversion term from *1 to *2. For example, <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) represents the baroclinic energy conversion from EAPE to EKE through vertical eddy heat fluxes. The terms with subscript <i>I</i> refer to interaction energies. Interaction energies are introduced to retain the mean-eddy cross terms required for local energy conservation (<a id="d7974185e3938" href="#bib44">Murakami 2011</a>). They do not constitute independent energy reservoirs as they vanish under the time mean but serve as intermediate energies mediating energy conversions between the local background flow and eddies. See <a id="d7974185e3941" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a> for a more detailed discussion on interaction energies and their conversions. The two interaction energies are defined as<div id="E19a" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M33"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math><label>(19a)</label></div><div id="E19b" class="formula"><math display="block" id="M34"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>υ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>υ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math><label>(19b)</label></div></div>
<p>Energy conversions involving interaction energies are interpreted as energy conversions from background flow, as both <i>A<sub>I</sub></i> and <i>K<sub>I</sub></i> are dominated by the mean component [overbar in Eq. <a id="ref_E19" href="#E19">(19)</a>] rather than the eddy component. For example, <i>C</i>(<i>K<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) is interpreted as KE conversion from the local background flow to eddies. Likewise, <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) is interpreted as APE conversion from the local background flow to eddies.</p>
<p>It should be noted that Eqs. <a id="d7974185e4055" href="#E15">(15)</a> and <a id="d7974185e4058" href="#E16">(16)</a> differ from the corresponding equations in <a id="d7974185e4061" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a> due to the use of the cyclone-following formulation. The divergence of EAPE and EKE, originally included in boundary flux terms, is transformed into advection terms using the continuity equation [Eq. <a id="ref_E4e" href="#E4e">(4e)</a>]. As a result, the EAPE boundary flux term vanishes, while the EKE boundary flux term is determined by the convergence of eddy geopotential flux alone. In addition, energy advection by background flow relative to TC movement appears as an advection term on the right-hand side of the equations.</p>
<p>Local eddy energetics in the cyclone frame of reference are schematized in <a id="d7974185e4070" href="#fig1">Fig. 1</a>. This study focuses on temporal and spatial changes in EAPE and EKE (<i>DA<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> and <i>DK<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i>). The key processes that determine the local eddy energies over a cyclone center, i.e., energy generation, dissipation, and conversions, are discussed in detail.
</p><div id="fig1" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f1.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 1."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-a979d099-c847-4db9-a435-323d2896a907" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f1.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 1."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-a979d099-c847-4db9-a435-323d2896a907">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f1.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 1."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 1.</figcaption>
<p>Box diagram of the local eddy energetics in the cyclone frame of reference. The boxes labeled <i>DA<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> and <i>DK<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> represent the tendencies of EAPE and EKE, respectively. The arrows labeled <i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>), <i>D</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>), and <i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) represent the generation of EAPE, the dissipation of EKE, and the boundary flux of EKE in the eddy energy equations [Eqs. <a id="d7974185e4132" href="#E15">(15)</a> and <a id="d7974185e4136" href="#E16">(16)</a>]. The arrow labeled <i>C</i>(*, *) represents the energy conversion terms, while the wavy arrows labeled <i>A</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) and <i>A</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) represent the energy advection by the background flow relative to the cyclone movement.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f1.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f1.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
Box diagram of the local eddy energetics in the cyclone frame of reference. The boxes labeled DAE/Dt and DKE/Dt represent the tendencies of EAPE and EKE, respectively. The arrows labeled G(AE), D(KE), and B(KE) represent the generation of EAPE, the dissipation of EKE, and the boundary flux of EKE in the eddy energy equations [Eqs. (15) and (16)]. The arrow labeled C(*, *) represents the energy conversion terms, while the wavy arrows labeled A(AE) and A(KE) represent the energy advection by the background flow relative to the cyclone movement." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
Box diagram of the local eddy energetics in the cyclone frame of reference. The boxes labeled DAE/Dt and DKE/Dt represent the tendencies of EAPE and EKE, respectively. The arrows labeled G(AE), D(KE), and B(KE) represent the generation of EAPE, the dissipation of EKE, and the boundary flux of EKE in the eddy energy equations [Eqs. (15) and (16)]. The arrow labeled C(*, *) represents the energy conversion terms, while the wavy arrows labeled A(AE) and A(KE) represent the energy advection by the background flow relative to the cyclone movement." data-header-text="Fig. 1." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f1.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p></div></div>
<div class="section border-bottom border-bottom-solid border-bottom-medium" id="s3">
<h2 id="d9691133e3262">3. Overview of Typhoon Hagibis</h2>
<p>We first present a life cycle overview of TC Hagibis (2019) in the western North Pacific. Its track, temporal evolution in the CPS diagram, and synoptic backgrounds are illustrated in <a id="ref_fig2" href="#fig2">Figs. 2</a>–<a id="ref_fig5" href="#fig5">5</a>. When the TC entered the midlatitudes, it underwent a structural change from a symmetric system to an asymmetric system (<a id="d7974185e4172" href="#bib39">Klein et al. 2000</a>; <a id="d7974185e4175" href="#bib19">Evans and Hart 2003</a>; <a id="d7974185e4178" href="#bib34">Jones et al. 2003</a>). The infrared satellite images clearly show such evolution (<a id="d7974185e4182" href="#fig2">Fig. 2</a>). The TC was well defined on 9 October (<a id="d7974185e4185" href="#fig2">Fig. 2a</a>). Both the TC eye and the symmetric spiral clouds wrapping around it were distinct. However, as the TC moved into the midlatitudes, a dry slot gradually enlarged and an elongated cloud extended northeastward, making the system asymmetric (<a id="d7974185e4188" href="#fig2">Figs. 2a–c</a>). Here, a dry slot results from dry and cold air descending from the upper levels to the rear of the surface cyclone. It indicates that the storm was under the influence of the midlatitude baroclinic environment (<a id="ref_bib9" href="#bib9">Browning 1997</a>; <a id="ref_bib10" href="#bib10">Carlson 1980</a>). The TC eye was no longer observed on 11 October, as the eyewall started to erode (<a id="d7974185e4197" href="#fig2">Fig. 2d</a>). Afterward, the asymmetry intensified, with a broad cirrus shield extending northeastward (<a id="d7974185e4201" href="#fig2">Figs. 2e,f</a>). <a id="d7974185e4204" href="#fig2">Figure 2f</a> shows a distinct dry slot intruding into the surface cyclone, revealing clear extratropical characteristics.
</p><div id="fig2" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f2.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 2."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-a5d0a78c-3b54-40c9-b44a-da09e3099e58" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f2.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 2."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-a5d0a78c-3b54-40c9-b44a-da09e3099e58">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f2.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 2."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 2.</figcaption>
<p>Satellite images (10.5-<i>μ</i>m infrared channel) obtained from GK2A at (a) 1100 UTC 9 Oct, (b) 0400 UTC 10 Oct, (c) 1800 UTC 10 Oct, (d) 0900 UTC 11 Oct, (e) 2100 UTC 11 Oct, and (f) 0500 UTC 12 Oct 2019.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f2.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f2.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
Satellite images (10.5-μm infrared channel) obtained from GK2A at (a) 1100 UTC 9 Oct, (b) 0400 UTC 10 Oct, (c) 1800 UTC 10 Oct, (d) 0900 UTC 11 Oct, (e) 2100 UTC 11 Oct, and (f) 0500 UTC 12 Oct 2019." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
Satellite images (10.5-μm infrared channel) obtained from GK2A at (a) 1100 UTC 9 Oct, (b) 0400 UTC 10 Oct, (c) 1800 UTC 10 Oct, (d) 0900 UTC 11 Oct, (e) 2100 UTC 11 Oct, and (f) 0500 UTC 12 Oct 2019." data-header-text="Fig. 2." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f2.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>The CPS diagram corroborates this structural change (<a id="ref_fig3" href="#fig3">Fig. 3</a>). TC Hagibis formed over the tropical ocean and moved rapidly westward until reaching 20°N (<a id="d7974185e4225" href="#fig3">Fig. 3a</a>). The storm then accelerated northward and recurved northeastward at 30.8°N. ET onset was identified at 0600 UTC 12 October, with the storm center located south of Honshu, the main island of Japan (<a id="d7974185e4228" href="#fig3">Figs. 3a,b</a>). The storm then made landfall, causing widespread damage (<a id="ref_bib32" href="#bib32">Iizuka et al. 2021</a>; <a id="d7974185e4234" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. 2022</a>), and subsequently moved over the open ocean northeast of Japan at 0000 UTC 13 October, marking ET completion. This was nearly coincident with the time at which the JMA best track first classified Hagibis as an extratropical cyclone (0300 UTC 13 October). Throughout the pretransition period, the TC maintained a deep warm-core structure (<a id="d7974185e4238" href="#fig3">Fig. 3c</a>). During ET, the storm transitioned into a shallow warm-core structure, eventually exhibiting a deep cold-core structure after ET completion. This evolution represents a canonical ET pathway, suggesting that TC Hagibis is a representative case for exploring ET dynamics through the local energetics framework.
</p><div id="fig3" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f3.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 3."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-ede43bf6-0c6c-483a-ab93-17350a15b9f8" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f3.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 3."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-ede43bf6-0c6c-483a-ab93-17350a15b9f8">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f3.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 3."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 3.</figcaption>
<p>(a) Track of TC Hagibis (2019) at 6-h intervals. (b) CPS diagram of 900–600-hPa thermal wind <inline-formula id="IE17" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM17"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> and thermal symmetry <i>B</i> for TC Hagibis (2019). (c) As in (b), but for 900–600-hPa thermal wind <inline-formula id="IE18" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM18"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> and 600–300-hPa thermal wind <inline-formula id="IE19" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM19"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula>. Red, orange, green, and blue dots indicate the beginning of the cyclone life cycle as a tropical storm, ET onset, ET completion, and the end of the JMA best track record, respectively.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f3.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f3.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
(a) Track of TC Hagibis (2019) at 6-h intervals. (b) CPS diagram of 900–600-hPa thermal wind −VTL and thermal symmetry B for TC Hagibis (2019). (c) As in (b), but for 900–600-hPa thermal wind −VTL and 600–300-hPa thermal wind −VTU. Red, orange, green, and blue dots indicate the beginning of the cyclone life cycle as a tropical storm, ET onset, ET completion, and the end of the JMA best track record, respectively." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
(a) Track of TC Hagibis (2019) at 6-h intervals. (b) CPS diagram of 900–600-hPa thermal wind −VTL and thermal symmetry B for TC Hagibis (2019). (c) As in (b), but for 900–600-hPa thermal wind −VTL and 600–300-hPa thermal wind −VTU. Red, orange, green, and blue dots indicate the beginning of the cyclone life cycle as a tropical storm, ET onset, ET completion, and the end of the JMA best track record, respectively." data-header-text="Fig. 3." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f3.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<div class="p">TC Hagibis was accompanied by a large amount of moisture transport, which is quantified by integrated water vapor transport from 1000 to 300 hPa:<div id="E19" class="formula"><math id="M35" display="block"><mtext>IVT</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msubsup><mi mathvariant="italic">uqdp</mi><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>hPa</mtext></mrow></msubsup><mi>υ</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">qdp</mi><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">y</mi><mo>,</mo></math><label>(19)</label></div>where <i>g</i> is the gravitational acceleration; <i>q</i> is the specific humidity; and <strong>x</strong> and <strong>y</strong> are the unit vectors in the zonal and meridional directions, respectively. In the TC stage, a strong cyclonic circulation was evident around the center, accompanied by counterclockwise moisture transport (<a id="d7974185e4349" href="#fig4">Fig. 4a</a>). In particular, in the right quadrant of the storm, stronger moisture transport appeared due to abundant moisture inflow from lower latitudes. In <a id="d7974185e4352" href="#fig4">Fig. 4b</a>, 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature <i>θ<sub>e</sub></i> was maximized at the cyclone center, reflecting the symmetric warm-core structure during the TC stage. This symmetric structure was maintained, but the 850-hPa geopotential height anomaly weakened and <i>θ<sub>e</sub></i> at the storm center decreased within 18 h (<a id="d7974185e4363" href="#fig4">Figs. 4c,d</a>). The central pressure in the best track indeed increased from 925 to 945 hPa during this period. Moisture transport not only continued to spiral counterclockwise around the center but also became more pronounced toward the northeast of the cyclone.
<div id="fig4" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f4.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 4."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-5aeb9061-05a5-4ec6-abcd-0f1d27fd814c" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f4.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 4."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-5aeb9061-05a5-4ec6-abcd-0f1d27fd814c">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f4.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 4."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 4.</figcaption>
<p>850-hPa geopotential height anomaly (shading; gpm), integrated water vapor transport (IVT) (vectors; &gt;300 kg m<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>), 250-hPa geopotential height (contours; gpm) at (a) 1800 UTC 10 Oct, (c) 1200 UTC 11 Oct, (e) 0600 UTC 12 Oct, and (g) 0000 UTC 13 Oct 2019. The boundary of the western North Pacific high is highlighted by a bold 5880-gpm contour. (b),(d),(f),(h) As in (a), (c), (e), and (g), but for 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature (shading; K), and the horizontal gradient of 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature [contours; for positive values starting from 6 K (100 km)<sup>−1</sup> with a 1 K (100 km)<sup>−1</sup> interval]. The red × in the (a), (c), (e), and (g) marks the TC center.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f4.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f4.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
850-hPa geopotential height anomaly (shading; gpm), integrated water vapor transport (IVT) (vectors; &gt;300 kg m−1 s−1), 250-hPa geopotential height (contours; gpm) at (a) 1800 UTC 10 Oct, (c) 1200 UTC 11 Oct, (e) 0600 UTC 12 Oct, and (g) 0000 UTC 13 Oct 2019. The boundary of the western North Pacific high is highlighted by a bold 5880-gpm contour. (b),(d),(f),(h) As in (a), (c), (e), and (g), but for 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature (shading; K), and the horizontal gradient of 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature [contours; for positive values starting from 6 K (100 km)−1 with a 1 K (100 km)−1 interval]. The red × in the (a), (c), (e), and (g) marks the TC center." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
850-hPa geopotential height anomaly (shading; gpm), integrated water vapor transport (IVT) (vectors; &gt;300 kg m−1 s−1), 250-hPa geopotential height (contours; gpm) at (a) 1800 UTC 10 Oct, (c) 1200 UTC 11 Oct, (e) 0600 UTC 12 Oct, and (g) 0000 UTC 13 Oct 2019. The boundary of the western North Pacific high is highlighted by a bold 5880-gpm contour. (b),(d),(f),(h) As in (a), (c), (e), and (g), but for 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature (shading; K), and the horizontal gradient of 850-hPa equivalent potential temperature [contours; for positive values starting from 6 K (100 km)−1 with a 1 K (100 km)−1 interval]. The red × in the (a), (c), (e), and (g) marks the TC center." data-header-text="Fig. 4." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f4.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div></div>
<p>In the ET stage, the asymmetry became more evident, as intensified winds in the right quadrant of the storm enhanced the poleward moisture transport (<a id="d7974185e4386" href="#fig4">Fig. 4e</a>). The prominent <i>θ<sub>e</sub></i> maximum, centered at the storm center, also became weakened (<a id="d7974185e4393" href="#fig4">Fig. 4f</a>). In the ETC stage, no pronounced <i>θ<sub>e</sub></i> maximum remained at the center (<a id="d7974185e4400" href="#fig4">Fig. 4h</a>), which is consistent with the negative <inline-formula id="IE20" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press"><math display="inline" id="IM20"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></inline-formula> observed in the CPS diagram (green dot in <a id="d7974185e4413" href="#fig3">Fig. 3b</a>), indicating that the storm transitioned into a cold-core system. Instead, <i>θ<sub>e</sub></i> exhibited a strong horizontal gradient along the Oyashio region, northeast of Japan. This is consistent with previous studies that have reported warm front development and associated precipitation in this region (<a id="d7974185e4420" href="#bib32">Iizuka et al. 2021</a>; <a id="d7974185e4423" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. 2022</a>). Moisture transport organized on the southeastern flank of the storm also reflects the transition into an asymmetric extratropical system (<a id="d7974185e4426" href="#fig4">Fig. 4g</a>).</p>
<p>It is noteworthy that no distinct upper-level trough developed either before or after the TC entered the midlatitude environment (<a id="d7974185e4433" href="#fig4">Figs. 4a,c,e,g</a>). An upper-level trough positioned northwest of a recurving TC represents a favorable condition for ET reintensification (<a id="d7974185e4436" href="#bib58">Ritchie and Elsberry 2003</a>, <a id="d7974185e4439" href="#bib59">2007</a>), but no notable trough–TC interaction occurred in the case of TC Hagibis. The track of TC Hagibis was likely steered by the western North Pacific high (<a id="d7974185e4442" href="#fig4">Figs. 4a,c,e,g</a>). Previous studies have shown that TCs in the western North Pacific are often steered by the western North Pacific high (e.g., <a id="ref_bib12" href="#bib12">Chan and Gray 1982</a>; <a id="ref_bib42" href="#bib42">Liu et al. 2018</a>), suggesting that the northeastward movement of TC Hagibis could be explained without notable interaction with an upper-level trough. The western North Pacific high played a further role in the eddy energy evolution of TC Hagibis during ET, as will be discussed in <a id="d7974185e4452" href="#s4">section 4</a>.</p>
<p>TC Hagibis exhibited a distinct ET evolution through jet–TC interaction (<a id="d7974185e4458" href="#fig5">Fig. 5</a>). In the TC stage, a symmetric upward motion was confined within the storm center (<a id="d7974185e4461" href="#fig5">Figs. 5a,b</a>). After 18 h, a strong westerly jet developed north of the TC (<a id="d7974185e4464" href="#fig5">Figs. 5c,d</a>). As the TC moved poleward, a northward-tilted asymmetric ascent emerged, suggesting the presence of jet–TC interaction. In the ET stage, the westerly jet further intensified, accompanied by a broad northeastward-tilted ascent region (<a id="d7974185e4467" href="#fig5">Figs. 5e,f</a>). An asymmetric structure in upward motion during ETC (<a id="d7974185e4470" href="#fig5">Figs. 5g,h</a>) further promoted asymmetric precipitation on the northeast flank of the cyclone (<a id="d7974185e4474" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. 2022</a>).
</p><div id="fig5" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f5.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 5."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-26a5e58a-a22f-48cd-9488-7ed1b16911c2" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f5.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 5."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-26a5e58a-a22f-48cd-9488-7ed1b16911c2">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f5.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 5."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 5.</figcaption>
<p>(a),(c),(e),(g) As in <a id="d7974185e4485" href="#fig4">Fig. 4</a>, but for 250-hPa horizontal wind speed (shading; m s<sup>−1</sup>) and 500-hPa omega (blue contours; for negative values with a 0.2 Pa s<sup>−1</sup> interval). (b),(d),(f),(h) As in (a), (c), (e), and (g), but for the latitude–pressure cross sections along the TC center for horizontal wind speed (shading; m s<sup>−1</sup>), omega (blue contours; for negative values with a 0.2 Pa s<sup>−1</sup> interval), and meridional–vertical wind (vectors; <i>ω</i> is multiplied by an aspect ratio of −21.46).</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f5.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f5.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
(a),(c),(e),(g) As in Fig. 4, but for 250-hPa horizontal wind speed (shading; m s−1) and 500-hPa omega (blue contours; for negative values with a 0.2 Pa s−1 interval). (b),(d),(f),(h) As in (a), (c), (e), and (g), but for the latitude–pressure cross sections along the TC center for horizontal wind speed (shading; m s−1), omega (blue contours; for negative values with a 0.2 Pa s−1 interval), and meridional–vertical wind (vectors; ω is multiplied by an aspect ratio of −21.46)." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
(a),(c),(e),(g) As in Fig. 4, but for 250-hPa horizontal wind speed (shading; m s−1) and 500-hPa omega (blue contours; for negative values with a 0.2 Pa s−1 interval). (b),(d),(f),(h) As in (a), (c), (e), and (g), but for the latitude–pressure cross sections along the TC center for horizontal wind speed (shading; m s−1), omega (blue contours; for negative values with a 0.2 Pa s−1 interval), and meridional–vertical wind (vectors; ω is multiplied by an aspect ratio of −21.46)." data-header-text="Fig. 5." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f5.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p><a id="d7974185e4503" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. (2022)</a> examined the jet–TC interaction process for TC Hagibis, dividing it into the prefrontal and frontal stages. During the early ET stage (prefrontal stage), the jet–TC interaction was attributed to reduced moist symmetric stability in the lower troposphere and midtroposphere along with conditional instability in the lower troposphere and inertial instability in the upper troposphere. In the lower troposphere, warm and moist air from lower latitudes resulted in conditional instability, establishing a thermodynamically favorable environment for ascent [Fig. 14b in <a id="d7974185e4506" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. (2022)</a>; see also <a id="d7974185e4509" href="#fig4">Figs. 4a</a>–<a id="d7974185e4512" href="#fig4">d</a> herein]. Above this layer, strong vertical shear, associated with an intensified meridional temperature gradient, reduced moist symmetric stability, promoting slantwise ascent along tilted isentropic surfaces [Figs. 14b,c in <a id="d7974185e4515" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. (2022)</a>; see also <a id="d7974185e4518" href="#fig5">Figs. 5a</a>–<a id="d7974185e4522" href="#fig5">d</a> herein]. Meanwhile, in the upper troposphere, poleward outflow from the TC was enhanced due to weakened inertial stability on the anticyclonic shear side (i.e., equatorward) of the westerly jet [Fig. 10b in <a id="d7974185e4525" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. (2022)</a>; see also <a id="d7974185e4528" href="#fig5">Figs. 5a</a>–<a id="d7974185e4531" href="#fig5">d</a> herein]. During the late ET and ETC stages (frontal stage), quasigeostrophic forcing associated with warm frontogenesis became dominant, enhancing the dynamical ascent and amplifying the asymmetry of the storm [Fig. 7d in <a id="d7974185e4534" href="#bib67">Yanase et al. (2022)</a>; see also <a id="d7974185e4537" href="#fig4">Figs. 4e–h</a> and <a id="d7974185e4541" href="#fig5">5e–h</a> herein]. This jet–TC interaction resulted in a pronounced asymmetric structure in TC Hagibis and related precipitation during its transition, even in the absence of a distinct upper-level trough.</p></div>
<div class="section border-bottom border-bottom-solid border-bottom-medium" id="s4">
<h2 id="d9691133e3648">4. Local energetics of Typhoon Hagibis</h2><div class="section" id="s4a">
<h3 id="d9691133e3652">a. Temporal evolution</h3>
<p>In this section, we demonstrate that the local energetics framework effectively captures the asymmetric features that emerge during the ET evolution of TC Hagibis. <a id="ref_fig6" href="#fig6">Figure 6</a> shows the evolution of vertically integrated EAPE (<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) and EKE (<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>). The vertical integration is conducted over the 1000–100-hPa layer, and storm-relative distributions are presented to examine structural changes around the storm.
</p><div id="fig6" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f6.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 6."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-ea6ae1a8-da45-4200-a1b3-e18d6a69ef19" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f6.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 6."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-ea6ae1a8-da45-4200-a1b3-e18d6a69ef19">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f6.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 6."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 6.</figcaption>
<p>Storm-relative distributions of vertically integrated (a),(c),(e) EAPE (<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) and (b),(d),(f) EKE (<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) at (a),(b) 1200 UTC 11 Oct 2019 (TC stage), (c),(d) 0600 UTC 12 Oct 2019 (ET stage), and (e),(f) 0000 UTC 13 Oct 2019 (ETC stage). The black circle denotes a 500-km radius from the TC center.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f6.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f6.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
Storm-relative distributions of vertically integrated (a),(c),(e) EAPE (AE) and (b),(d),(f) EKE (KE) at (a),(b) 1200 UTC 11 Oct 2019 (TC stage), (c),(d) 0600 UTC 12 Oct 2019 (ET stage), and (e),(f) 0000 UTC 13 Oct 2019 (ETC stage). The black circle denotes a 500-km radius from the TC center." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
Storm-relative distributions of vertically integrated (a),(c),(e) EAPE (AE) and (b),(d),(f) EKE (KE) at (a),(b) 1200 UTC 11 Oct 2019 (TC stage), (c),(d) 0600 UTC 12 Oct 2019 (ET stage), and (e),(f) 0000 UTC 13 Oct 2019 (ETC stage). The black circle denotes a 500-km radius from the TC center." data-header-text="Fig. 6." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f6.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>The EAPE is maximum near the storm center in the TC stage, reflecting strong local temperature perturbations (<a id="d7974185e4589" href="#fig6">Fig. 6a</a>). As the storm undergoes ET and becomes ETC, thermal perturbations are enhanced while being redistributed asymmetrically (<a id="d7974185e4592" href="#fig6">Figs. 6c,e</a>). The EKE also evolves markedly. In the TC stage, its distribution is zonally symmetric but meridionally asymmetric with a maximum to the north of the storm center (<a id="d7974185e4595" href="#fig6">Fig. 6b</a>). It subsequently decreases in magnitude and becomes zonally asymmetric in the ET and ETC stages, with a pronounced maximum to the east of the storm center (<a id="d7974185e4598" href="#fig6">Figs. 6d,f</a>).</p>
<p><a id="ref_fig7" href="#fig7">Figure 7</a> summarizes the temporal evolution of eddy energies within a 500-km radius around TC Hagibis. The 500-km radius is chosen here to encompass the characteristic scales of both TC and ETC systems and to maintain consistency with the CPS-based ET criteria. The results are robust to the choice of radius, although the overall magnitudes increase with a decreasing radius (e.g., from 600 to 500 km and to 400 km). Total eddy energy, which is the sum of EAPE and EKE, increases until 1200 UTC 11 October due to a rapid increase in EKE and a rather slow increase in EAPE. Afterward, EAPE continues to increase at an even faster rate until 1200 UTC 13 October. However, EKE sharply decreases at a rate exceeding its prior increase. This leaves the total eddy energy relatively unchanged from the late TC stage (last day of the green shaded period) to ET completion (second vertical dashed line). After ET completion at 0000 UTC 13 October, total eddy energy slightly increases and then decreases.
</p><div id="fig7" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f7.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 7."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-e2da3b67-61cf-4c6b-991a-fbcacfee839a" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f7.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 7."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-e2da3b67-61cf-4c6b-991a-fbcacfee839a">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f7.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 7."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 7.</figcaption>
<p>Time series of vertically integrated total eddy energy (<i>A<sub>E</sub></i> + <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>; gray line), EAPE (<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>; blue line), and EKE (<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>; green line) for TC Hagibis (2019), averaged within a 500-km radius around the TC center. Dashed gray lines denote the onset and completion of ET as defined by the CPS diagram. Green and blue shadings indicate the TC and ET phases, respectively.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f7.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f7.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
Time series of vertically integrated total eddy energy (AE + KE; gray line), EAPE (AE; blue line), and EKE (KE; green line) for TC Hagibis (2019), averaged within a 500-km radius around the TC center. Dashed gray lines denote the onset and completion of ET as defined by the CPS diagram. Green and blue shadings indicate the TC and ET phases, respectively." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
Time series of vertically integrated total eddy energy (AE + KE; gray line), EAPE (AE; blue line), and EKE (KE; green line) for TC Hagibis (2019), averaged within a 500-km radius around the TC center. Dashed gray lines denote the onset and completion of ET as defined by the CPS diagram. Green and blue shadings indicate the TC and ET phases, respectively." data-header-text="Fig. 7." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f7.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>This result suggests that local energetics undergo marked changes from the TC stage to the ET stage and then to the ETC stage. To characterize TC and ET energetics, two periods are examined in detail below. The first period is from 0600 UTC 10 October to 1200 UTC 11 October, when both EAPE and EKE increase (green shading in <a id="d7974185e4634" href="#fig7">Fig. 7</a>). This period is defined as the TC phase. The second period is from 0600 UTC 12 October to 1200 UTC 13 October, when EAPE increases but EKE decreases (blue shading in <a id="d7974185e4637" href="#fig7">Fig. 7</a>). This period is defined as the extended ET phase (hereafter simply ET phase). It encompasses the CPS-based ET and early ETC stages and represents an energetically defined ET stage.</p>
<p><a id="ref_fig8" href="#fig8">Figure 8</a> shows the vertically integrated EAPE and EKE tendency budgets during the TC and ET phases. All budget terms maintain the same signs from the TC to ET phases, but their magnitudes increase. The only exception is the EKE dissipation term [<i>D</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)] in <a id="d7974185e4653" href="#fig8">Fig. 8b</a>. Unlike individual budget terms, the total tendency of EKE switches from positive in the TC phase to negative in the ET phase (first two bars in <a id="d7974185e4656" href="#fig8">Fig. 8b</a>). This result indicates that the energy cycle becomes stronger in the ET phase, accompanied by an EKE decrease. The exact values for each term and their positions in the energy cycle are summarized later (see <a id="ref_fig16" href="#fig16">Fig. 16</a>).
</p><div id="fig8" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f8.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 8."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-d5149e2d-1d69-4ade-867d-7b4486a5c65b" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f8.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 8."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-d5149e2d-1d69-4ade-867d-7b4486a5c65b">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f8.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 8."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 8.</figcaption>
<p>(a) The vertically integrated EAPE tendency (<i>DA<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i>; black bar; left <i>y</i> axis) derived from EAPE budget [Eq. <a id="d7974185e4681" href="#E16">(16)</a>], consisting of EAPE generation [<i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>); red bar; right <i>y</i> axis], EAPE conversion from background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>); yellow bar; right <i>y</i> axis], baroclinic energy conversion from EKE to EAPE [−<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>); green bar; right <i>y</i> axis], EAPE advection [<i>A</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>); blue bar; right <i>y</i> axis], and residual (pink bar; right <i>y</i> axis). (b) As in (a), but for the EKE tendency (<i>DK<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i>; black bar) derived from EKE budget [Eq. <a id="d7974185e4745" href="#E15">(15)</a>], consisting of boundary flux [<i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>); red bar], dissipation [<i>D</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>); orange bar], EKE conversion from background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>K<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>); yellow bar], baroclinic energy conversion from EAPE to EKE [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>); green bar], EKE advection [<i>A</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>); blue bar], and residual (pink bar). All values are averaged within a 500-km radius around the TC center. Solid bars and hatched bars indicate TC and ET phases, respectively. Note that the ranges of the left and right <i>y</i> axes differ.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f8.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f8.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
(a) The vertically integrated EAPE tendency (DAE/Dt; black bar; left y axis) derived from EAPE budget [Eq. (16)], consisting of EAPE generation [G(AE); red bar; right y axis], EAPE conversion from background flow [C(AI, AE); yellow bar; right y axis], baroclinic energy conversion from EKE to EAPE [−C(AE, KE); green bar; right y axis], EAPE advection [A(AE); blue bar; right y axis], and residual (pink bar; right y axis). (b) As in (a), but for the EKE tendency (DKE/Dt; black bar) derived from EKE budget [Eq. (15)], consisting of boundary flux [B(KE); red bar], dissipation [D(KE); orange bar], EKE conversion from background flow [C(KI, KE); yellow bar], baroclinic energy conversion from EAPE to EKE [C(AE, KE); green bar], EKE advection [A(KE); blue bar], and residual (pink bar). All values are averaged within a 500-km radius around the TC center. Solid bars and hatched bars indicate TC and ET phases, respectively. Note that the ranges of the left and right y axes differ." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
(a) The vertically integrated EAPE tendency (DAE/Dt; black bar; left y axis) derived from EAPE budget [Eq. (16)], consisting of EAPE generation [G(AE); red bar; right y axis], EAPE conversion from background flow [C(AI, AE); yellow bar; right y axis], baroclinic energy conversion from EKE to EAPE [−C(AE, KE); green bar; right y axis], EAPE advection [A(AE); blue bar; right y axis], and residual (pink bar; right y axis). (b) As in (a), but for the EKE tendency (DKE/Dt; black bar) derived from EKE budget [Eq. (15)], consisting of boundary flux [B(KE); red bar], dissipation [D(KE); orange bar], EKE conversion from background flow [C(KI, KE); yellow bar], baroclinic energy conversion from EAPE to EKE [C(AE, KE); green bar], EKE advection [A(KE); blue bar], and residual (pink bar). All values are averaged within a 500-km radius around the TC center. Solid bars and hatched bars indicate TC and ET phases, respectively. Note that the ranges of the left and right y axes differ." data-header-text="Fig. 8." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f8.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>The EAPE increase is mainly caused by its generation [<i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)] and the APE conversion from background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)], while it decreases through the baroclinic energy conversion [−<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)] (<a id="d7974185e4830" href="#fig8">Fig. 8a</a>). In other words, EAPE of the storm increases through diabatic heating and warm temperature anomalies [<i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)] and thermodynamic energy conversion from background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)]. This is partly offset by the energy conversion from EAPE to EKE [−<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)]. The sum of EAPE generation [<i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)] and energy conversion from background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)] is slightly larger than the baroclinic energy conversion [−<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)], leading to a positive tendency of EAPE (leftmost bars in <a id="d7974185e4894" href="#fig8">Fig. 8a</a>), although the residual is nonnegligible (note that the difference in the ranges of the left and right <i>y</i> axes). The residual is possibly attributed to the temporal mismatch between local tendency and advection terms in the 6-hourly dataset, as well as other numerical errors. As the storm undergoes ET, all processes intensify. Among them, APE conversion from background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)] is more than doubled, contributing to a stronger EAPE change in the ET phase compared to the TC phase.</p>
<p>The EKE shows a different life cycle. It increases in the TC phase but decreases in the ET phase (leftmost bars in <a id="d7974185e4915" href="#fig8">Fig. 8b</a>). This is caused by the lateral boundary flux [<i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)] exceeding the baroclinic energy conversion [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)] in the ET phase (first and fourth budget terms in <a id="d7974185e4937" href="#fig8">Fig. 8b</a>). This contrasts with the TC phase, where the EKE increase due to baroclinic energy conversion is partly offset by the lateral boundary flux. The energy conversion from the background flow [<i>C</i>(<i>K<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>)] plays only a minor role. Here, it should be noted that the baroclinic energy conversion does not necessarily indicate baroclinic instability as this term is associated with vertical heat flux instead of meridional heat flux [Eq. <a id="ref_E17b" href="#E17b">(17b)</a>].</p></div>
<div class="section" id="s4b">
<h3 id="d9691133e4056">b. Spatial distribution of major terms</h3>
<p>The above results suggest that during the ET phase, EAPE increases due to enhanced diabatic heating and energy conversion from background flow, while EKE decreases due to dominant boundary flux divergence, which overwhelms enhanced baroclinic energy conversion. <a id="d7974185e4963" href="#fig9">Figure 9</a> further presents the spatial distribution of four major terms that determine eddy energy changes. In the TC phase, all terms show symmetric patterns about the storm center (<a id="d7974185e4966" href="#fig9">Figs. 9a–d</a>). They become asymmetric in the ET phase (<a id="d7974185e4969" href="#fig9">Figs. 9e–h</a>). The increase in magnitude and spatial asymmetry of energy terms during the ET phase largely results from the interaction of the cyclone with the midlatitude baroclinic environment.
</p><div id="fig9" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f9.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 9."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-0a3442bd-dd06-44d7-b943-7dc8c1c6b076" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f9.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 9."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-0a3442bd-dd06-44d7-b943-7dc8c1c6b076">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f9.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 9."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 9.</figcaption>
<p>Storm-relative composites of vertically integrated (a),(e) <i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>), (b),(f) <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>), (c),(g) <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>), and (d),(h) <i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) during (a)–(d) TC phase and (e)–(h) ET phase. The black circle denotes a 500-km radius from the TC center.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f9.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f9.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
Storm-relative composites of vertically integrated (a),(e) G(AE), (b),(f) C(AI, AE), (c),(g) C(AE, KE), and (d),(h) B(KE) during (a)–(d) TC phase and (e)–(h) ET phase. The black circle denotes a 500-km radius from the TC center." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
Storm-relative composites of vertically integrated (a),(e) G(AE), (b),(f) C(AI, AE), (c),(g) C(AE, KE), and (d),(h) B(KE) during (a)–(d) TC phase and (e)–(h) ET phase. The black circle denotes a 500-km radius from the TC center." data-header-text="Fig. 9." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f9.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p><a id="ref_fig10" href="#fig10">Figure 10</a> shows the storm-relative composite of vertically integrated EAPE generation [<i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>)]. Note that <a id="d7974185e5030" href="#fig10">Figs. 10a</a> and <a id="d7974185e5033" href="#fig10">10d</a> are identical to <a id="d7974185e5036" href="#fig9">Figs. 9a</a> and <a id="d7974185e5040" href="#fig9">9e</a> and shown for comparison with the spatial structures of diabatic heating and temperature anomalies in the middle and right columns of <a id="d7974185e5043" href="#fig10">Fig. 10</a>. Here, the term “anomaly” is used to denote the eddy component, which is defined by removing the 30-day running mean. In the TC phase, <i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) shows an overall symmetric pattern with a maximum near the storm center although slightly shifted northeastward (<a id="d7974185e5053" href="#fig10">Fig. 10a</a>). This pattern results from the combined effects of diabatic heating and temperature anomalies [Eq. <a id="ref_E18a" href="#E18a">(18a)</a>]. By definition, EAPE increases [<i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) &gt; 0] through diabatic heating and warm temperature anomalies within the storm. The diabatic heating anomaly exhibits a slightly eastward-shifted distribution (<a id="d7974185e5067" href="#fig10">Fig. 10b</a>). This pattern, not exactly centered at the storm center, is due to convective heating. Unlike condensational heating, which is centered at the storm center (<a id="ref_fig11" href="#fig11">Fig. 11b</a>), convective heating is centered to the east of the storm center (<a id="d7974185e5073" href="#fig11">Fig. 11a</a>) where the moisture supply is stronger (<a id="d7974185e5076" href="#fig4">Fig. 4c</a>). Temperature anomaly is controlled by diabatic heating and temperature advection. Warm advection is enhanced in the northeast quadrant of the storm (<a id="d7974185e5080" href="#fig11">Fig. 11c</a>). Despite this enhancement and an eastward-shifted diabatic heating (<a id="d7974185e5083" href="#fig10">Fig. 10b</a>), their combination maintains an overall symmetric pattern when integrated in the vertical (<a id="d7974185e5086" href="#fig10">Fig. 10c</a>).
</p><div id="fig10" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f10.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 10."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-ac847302-3c9a-40e6-ba17-7a065d3b5250" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f10.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 10."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-ac847302-3c9a-40e6-ba17-7a065d3b5250">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f10.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 10."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 10.</figcaption>
<p>As in <a id="d7974185e5097" href="#fig9">Fig. 9</a>, but for vertically integrated (a),(d) <i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) (W m<sup>−2</sup>), (b),(e) vertically averaged diabatic heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)<sup>−1</sup>], and (c),(f) vertically averaged temperature anomaly (K).</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f10.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f10.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for vertically integrated (a),(d) G(AE) (W m−2), (b),(e) vertically averaged diabatic heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)−1], and (c),(f) vertically averaged temperature anomaly (K)." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for vertically integrated (a),(d) G(AE) (W m−2), (b),(e) vertically averaged diabatic heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)−1], and (c),(f) vertically averaged temperature anomaly (K)." data-header-text="Fig. 10." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f10.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div>
<div id="fig11" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f11.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 11."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-b915e0df-7518-4e41-ab75-32e52e8bd30a" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f11.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 11."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-b915e0df-7518-4e41-ab75-32e52e8bd30a">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f11.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 11."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 11.</figcaption>
<p>As in <a id="d7974185e5121" href="#fig9">Fig. 9</a>, but for (a),(d) vertically averaged convective heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)<sup>−1</sup>], (b),(e) vertically averaged large-scale condensation heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)<sup>−1</sup>], and (c),(f) vertically averaged temperature advection (shading; 10<sup>−4</sup> K s<sup>−1</sup>) and vertically averaged horizontal wind anomaly (vectors; m s<sup>−1</sup>).</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f11.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f11.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically averaged convective heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)−1], (b),(e) vertically averaged large-scale condensation heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)−1], and (c),(f) vertically averaged temperature advection (shading; 10−4 K s−1) and vertically averaged horizontal wind anomaly (vectors; m s−1)." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically averaged convective heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)−1], (b),(e) vertically averaged large-scale condensation heating rate anomaly [K (6 h)−1], and (c),(f) vertically averaged temperature advection (shading; 10−4 K s−1) and vertically averaged horizontal wind anomaly (vectors; m s−1)." data-header-text="Fig. 11." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f11.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>In the ET phase, <i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) intensifies strongly with a distinct asymmetric structure extending northeastward (<a id="d7974185e5146" href="#fig10">Fig. 10d</a>). Diabatic heating is enhanced to the north of the storm and extends northeastward (<a id="d7974185e5149" href="#fig10">Fig. 10e</a>), due to large-scale condensation (<a id="d7974185e5152" href="#fig11">Fig. 11e</a>). This pattern is associated with enhanced precipitation processes northeast of the storm under the jet–TC interaction (<a id="d7974185e5156" href="#fig5">Figs. 5e,f</a>). Temperature anomaly also shows a notable enhancement to the northeast of the storm (<a id="d7974185e5159" href="#fig10">Fig. 10f</a>). It reflects both the northeastward-extended diabatic heating and the temperature advection. As shown in <a id="d7974185e5162" href="#fig11">Fig. 11f</a>, temperature advection is characterized by warm advection ahead and cold advection in the rear of the storm. This pattern is a typical feature of a midlatitude cyclone (e.g., <a id="ref_bib41" href="#bib41">Lackmann 2015</a>; <a id="ref_bib11" href="#bib11">Catto et al. 2010</a>) and creates the asymmetry in ET temperature anomaly shown in <a id="d7974185e5171" href="#fig10">Fig. 10f</a>. This suggests that the intensification and strong asymmetry of <i>G</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) during the ET phase (first bars in the right-hand column of <a id="d7974185e5182" href="#fig8">Fig. 8a</a>) result from enhanced diabatic heating and asymmetric temperature advection due to interactions between the storm and background flow in midlatitudes.</p>
<p>The <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>), APE conversion from background flow to eddy, shows an asymmetric pattern especially in the ET phase (<a id="ref_fig12" href="#fig12">Figs. 12a,d</a>). These figures are again identical to <a id="d7974185e5202" href="#fig9">Figs. 9b</a> and <a id="d7974185e5206" href="#fig9">9f</a> and shown here for reference. The spatial pattern of this term is decomposed into the three components, i.e., zonal, meridional, and vertical heat fluxes [Eq. <a id="ref_E18b" href="#E18b">(18b)</a>]. It is dominated by vertical heat flux in the TC phase (<a id="d7974185e5212" href="#fig12">Figs. 12b,c</a>) but by meridional heat flux in the ET phase (<a id="d7974185e5215" href="#fig12">Figs. 12e,f</a>). In the TC phase, upward heat flux (<i>ω</i>′<i>θ</i>′ &lt; 0) weakens the vertical temperature gradient east of the storm, facilitating APE conversion from background flow to eddy [<i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) &gt; 0; <a id="d7974185e5236" href="#fig12">Fig. 12a</a>]. In the ET phase, <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) intensifies to the northeastward of the storm (<a id="d7974185e5251" href="#fig12">Fig. 12d</a>). This is because the cyclonic circulation, maximized on the east of the storm center, drives an enhanced meridional eddy heat flux under the stronger background meridional temperature gradient (<a id="d7974185e5254" href="#fig12">Figs. 12e,f</a>). The enhanced meridional eddy heat flux reduces the background potential temperature gradient, leading to the decrease in background APE and the increase in EAPE. This explains positive <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) (<a id="d7974185e5269" href="#fig12">Figs. 12a,d</a>) in both the TC and ET phases, which increases from the TC to ET phases (second bars in the right-hand column of <a id="d7974185e5272" href="#fig8">Fig. 8a</a>).
</p><div id="fig12" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f12.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 12."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-dd4da987-7195-46c5-8150-094d0c801524" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f12.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 12."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-dd4da987-7195-46c5-8150-094d0c801524">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f12.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 12."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 12.</figcaption>
<p>As in <a id="d7974185e5283" href="#fig9">Fig. 9</a>, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) (W m<sup>−2</sup>), (b) vertical component of vertically integrated <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) (shading; W m<sup>−2</sup>) and vertically averaged <i>p</i>-velocity anomaly (contours; with a 0.1 Pa s<sup>−1</sup> interval), (c) vertically averaged vertical eddy heat flux (contours; with a 1 K Pa s<sup>−1</sup> interval) and vertically averaged vertical (<inline-formula id="IE21"><math display="inline" id="IM21"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></math></inline-formula>) gradient (shading; 10<sup>−4</sup> K Pa<sup>−1</sup>), (e) meridional component of vertically integrated <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) (shading; W m<sup>−2</sup>) and vertically averaged horizontal wind anomaly (vectors; m s<sup>−1</sup>), and (f) vertically averaged meridional eddy heat flux (contours; with a 30 K m s<sup>−1</sup> interval) and vertically averaged meridional (<inline-formula id="IE22"><math display="inline" id="IM22"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></math></inline-formula>) gradient (shading; 10<sup>−6</sup> K m<sup>−1</sup>).</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f12.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f12.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated C(AI, AE) (W m−2), (b) vertical component of vertically integrated C(AI, AE) (shading; W m−2) and vertically averaged p-velocity anomaly (contours; with a 0.1 Pa s−1 interval), (c) vertically averaged vertical eddy heat flux (contours; with a 1 K Pa s−1 interval) and vertically averaged vertical (θ¯−⟨θ¯⟩) gradient (shading; 10−4 K Pa−1), (e) meridional component of vertically integrated C(AI, AE) (shading; W m−2) and vertically averaged horizontal wind anomaly (vectors; m s−1), and (f) vertically averaged meridional eddy heat flux (contours; with a 30 K m s−1 interval) and vertically averaged meridional (θ¯−⟨θ¯⟩) gradient (shading; 10−6 K m−1)." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated C(AI, AE) (W m−2), (b) vertical component of vertically integrated C(AI, AE) (shading; W m−2) and vertically averaged p-velocity anomaly (contours; with a 0.1 Pa s−1 interval), (c) vertically averaged vertical eddy heat flux (contours; with a 1 K Pa s−1 interval) and vertically averaged vertical (θ¯−⟨θ¯⟩) gradient (shading; 10−4 K Pa−1), (e) meridional component of vertically integrated C(AI, AE) (shading; W m−2) and vertically averaged horizontal wind anomaly (vectors; m s−1), and (f) vertically averaged meridional eddy heat flux (contours; with a 30 K m s−1 interval) and vertically averaged meridional (θ¯−⟨θ¯⟩) gradient (shading; 10−6 K m−1)." data-header-text="Fig. 12." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f12.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>Similar results are found in the baroclinic energy conversion term <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>), which is one of the key terms that determine both EAPE and EKE changes (<a id="d7974185e5392" href="#fig8">Fig. 8</a>). In the TC phase, this term exhibits a symmetric pattern concentrated near the storm center with a slight northeastward extension (<a id="ref_fig13" href="#fig13">Fig. 13a</a>; identical to <a id="d7974185e5399" href="#fig9">Fig. 9c</a>). By contrast, in the ET phase, it shows a significant asymmetric pattern with a northeast–southwest dipole structure (<a id="d7974185e5402" href="#fig13">Fig. 13d</a>; identical to <a id="d7974185e5405" href="#fig9">Fig. 9g</a>). This term essentially represents the vertical heat flux [Eq. <a id="d7974185e5408" href="#E17b">(17b)</a>], which is determined by a specific volume anomaly centered at the storm center (<a id="d7974185e5411" href="#fig13">Figs. 13c,f</a>) and <i>p</i>-velocity anomaly whose center changes from the TC to ET phases (<a id="d7974185e5418" href="#fig13">Figs. 13b,e</a>). The spatial pattern of the former resembles that of temperature anomaly (<a id="d7974185e5421" href="#fig10">Figs. 10c,f</a>), as specific volume is directly related to temperature on isobaric surface through the ideal gas law. The latter is also related to the diabatic heating anomaly (<a id="d7974185e5424" href="#fig10">Figs. 10b,e</a>), as latent heating, which accounts for most of the diabatic heating, needs to accompany strong upward motion.
</p><div id="fig13" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f13.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 13."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-923635c7-bf4e-4a63-b359-84f1a6b30991" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f13.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 13."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-923635c7-bf4e-4a63-b359-84f1a6b30991">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f13.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 13."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 13.</figcaption>
<p>As in <a id="d7974185e5435" href="#fig9">Fig. 9</a>, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) (W m<sup>−2</sup>), (b),(e) vertically averaged <i>p</i>-velocity anomaly (Pa s<sup>−1</sup>), and (c),(f) vertically averaged specific volume anomaly (10<sup>−2</sup> m<sup>3</sup> kg<sup>−1</sup>).</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f13.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f13.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated C(AE, KE) (W m−2), (b),(e) vertically averaged p-velocity anomaly (Pa s−1), and (c),(f) vertically averaged specific volume anomaly (10−2 m3 kg−1)." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated C(AE, KE) (W m−2), (b),(e) vertically averaged p-velocity anomaly (Pa s−1), and (c),(f) vertically averaged specific volume anomaly (10−2 m3 kg−1)." data-header-text="Fig. 13." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f13.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>In the northeast quadrant of the ET, positive <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) reaches its maximum and extends northeastward (<a id="d7974185e5478" href="#fig13">Fig. 13d</a>). This indicates strong energy conversion from EAPE to EKE. In the southwest quadrant of the ET, negative <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) extends westward, showing the opposite energy conversion. This dipole pattern results from enhanced upward motion ahead of the storm and downward motion in the rear of the storm (<a id="d7974185e5493" href="#fig13">Fig. 13e</a>), which is a common feature of the ETC (e.g., <a id="d7974185e5496" href="#bib41">Lackmann 2015</a>; <a id="d7974185e5499" href="#bib11">Catto et al. 2010</a>). The associated thermodynamically and dynamically driven ascent is likely enhanced under the jet–TC interaction, as discussed earlier. This may explain enhanced large-scale condensation in the ET phase (<a id="d7974185e5502" href="#fig11">Fig. 11e</a>). It should be noted that in midlatitudes, <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) can be positive even in the rear of the storm due to downward motion accompanied by a cold anomaly. The dipole pattern shown in the present study likely reflects the relatively warm air associated with the TC that persists even during the ET and ETC stages (<a id="d7974185e5517" href="#fig4">Figs. 4f,h</a>). It should also be noted that <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) and the vertical component of <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>) are physically linked. The upward eddy heat flux converts EAPE to EKE through <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>E</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>E</sub></i>), while simultaneously reducing the vertical temperature gradient and driving APE conversion from the background flow to the eddy through <i>C</i>(<i>A<sub>I</sub></i>, <i>A<sub>E</sub></i>).</p>
<p>The lateral boundary flux <i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) is the key factor in determining EKE change in the ET phase (<a id="d7974185e5576" href="#fig8">Figs. 8b</a> and <a id="ref_fig14" href="#fig14">14</a>). This term is quantified by eddy geopotential flux convergence [Eq. <a id="ref_E17a" href="#E17a">(17a)</a>] and represents spatial redistribution of EKE, rather than its generation or dissipation. Its divergence weakens EKE, whereas the convergence strengthens EKE [Eq. <a id="d7974185e5586" href="#E15">(15)</a>]. The <i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) is dominated by the horizontal eddy geopotential flux divergence during the TC and ET phases (<a id="d7974185e5596" href="#fig14">Fig. 14</a>). In the TC phase, eddy geopotential flux divergence occurs along the northeast–southwest axis and convergence in the northwest and southeast quadrants outside the storm (<a id="d7974185e5599" href="#fig14">Fig. 14b</a>). Within the 500-km radius, eddy geopotential flux diverges radially [<i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) &lt; 0 in <a id="d7974185e5610" href="#fig8">Fig. 8b</a>], indicating a net outward transport of EKE away from the storm center (<a id="d7974185e5613" href="#fig14">Fig. 14c</a>). In the ET phase, eddy geopotential flux divergence is enhanced in the northeast quadrant, while convergence occurs in the southwest quadrant (<a id="d7974185e5616" href="#fig14">Fig. 14e</a>). This is due to a background anticyclonic circulation anomaly that appears to the northeast of the storm. This circulation, which is associated with the western North Pacific high in the lower–midtroposphere, enhances eddy geopotential flux divergence in the northeast quadrant of the storm (<a id="d7974185e5619" href="#fig14">Fig. 14f</a>). In the upper troposphere, the divergent TC outflow associated with the jet–TC interaction also contributes to the redistribution of EKE. Together, these processes highlight the role of the large-scale midlatitude environment in redistributing EKE around the storm. This outward transport of EKE may be linked to the wind field expansion typically observed during ET (e.g., <a id="d7974185e5622" href="#bib17">Evans and Hart 2008</a>). However, a clear expansion of the EKE distribution is not evident in our results (<a id="d7974185e5626" href="#fig6">Fig. 6f</a>), likely due to the offset by baroclinic energy conversion (<a id="d7974185e5629" href="#fig9">Figs. 9g,h</a>).
</p><div id="fig14" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f14.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 14."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-6922f626-fb14-42d8-ad9d-08ef1092a728" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
