	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f14.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 14."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-6922f626-fb14-42d8-ad9d-08ef1092a728">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f14.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 14."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 14.</figcaption>
<p>As in <a id="d7974185e5640" href="#fig9">Fig. 9</a>, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated <i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) (W m<sup>−2</sup>), (b),(e) horizontal component of vertically integrated <i>B</i>(<i>K<sub>E</sub></i>) (W m<sup>−2</sup>), and (c),(f) vertically averaged eddy geopotential flux (vectors; m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>−3</sup>) and vertically averaged geopotential anomaly (shading; m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>−2</sup>).</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f14.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f14.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated B(KE) (W m−2), (b),(e) horizontal component of vertically integrated B(KE) (W m−2), and (c),(f) vertically averaged eddy geopotential flux (vectors; m3 s−3) and vertically averaged geopotential anomaly (shading; m2 s−2)." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
As in Fig. 9, but for (a),(d) vertically integrated B(KE) (W m−2), (b),(e) horizontal component of vertically integrated B(KE) (W m−2), and (c),(f) vertically averaged eddy geopotential flux (vectors; m3 s−3) and vertically averaged geopotential anomaly (shading; m2 s−2)." data-header-text="Fig. 14." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f14.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p></div></div>
<div class="section border-bottom border-bottom-solid border-bottom-medium" id="s5">
<h2 id="d9691133e4772">5. Summary and discussion</h2>
<p>This study proposes a local energetics framework to examine ET dynamics and demonstrates its usefulness by applying it to TC Hagibis (2019) in the western North Pacific. TC Hagibis (2019) showed marked extratropical characteristics during its transition, as clearly captured in the CPS diagram. The storm exhibited a deep warm core and symmetric structure during the TC stage, evolved into an increasingly asymmetric system during the ET stage, and ultimately developed a deep cold core during the ETC stage. As the storm moved into the midlatitudes, asymmetries in low-level circulation, moisture transport, and associated upward motion became more apparent. This distinct ET evolution was primarily driven by the jet–TC interaction, in which the strong vertical wind shear and the anticyclonic shear of the upper-level jet enhanced slantwise ascent and strengthened the TC outflow. The quasigeostrophic ascent on the equatorward side of the jet entrance region also likely contributed to a pronounced asymmetric structure during ET.</p>
<p>The energy cycle of TC Hagibis is quantified using the local energetics proposed by <a id="d7974185e5682" href="#bib44">Murakami (2011)</a>. The original formulation is modified for use in the cyclone frame of reference (or cyclone-following framework). All budget terms are vertically integrated over the 1000–100-hPa layer and averaged within a 500-km radius of the storm center. Local energetics undergo marked changes from the TC to ET phases. All budget terms maintain the same sign from the TC to ET phases, but their magnitudes increase except for the EKE dissipation term. During the TC phase (green line in <a id="ref_fig15" href="#fig15">Fig. 15</a>; top panel of <a id="d7974185e5688" href="#fig16">Fig. 16</a>), EAPE increases (<i>DA<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> &gt; 0 in <a id="d7974185e5699" href="#fig15">Fig. 15</a>) as its generation and conversion from background flow dominate over the baroclinic energy conversion, whereas EKE increases slightly on average (<i>DK<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> &gt; 0 in <a id="d7974185e5709" href="#fig15">Fig. 15</a>) as baroclinic energy conversion dominates over lateral boundary flux. During the ET phase (blue line in <a id="d7974185e5712" href="#fig15">Fig. 15</a>; bottom panel of <a id="d7974185e5715" href="#fig16">Fig. 16</a>), as the storm moves into the midlatitudes, EAPE increases (<i>DA<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> &gt; 0 in <a id="d7974185e5726" href="#fig15">Fig. 15</a>) due to enhanced diabatic heating and energy conversion from background flow. In particular, the energy conversion from background flow more than doubles relative to the TC phase, contributing substantially to the EAPE increase. However, EKE decreases significantly (<i>DK<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i> &lt; 0 in <a id="d7974185e5736" href="#fig15">Fig. 15</a>). This reduction results from strong lateral boundary flux divergence, which is influenced by large-scale circulation in the mid–lower levels (e.g., the western North Pacific subtropical high) and divergent TC outflow in the upper levels, despite enhanced baroclinic energy conversion. The energy budget terms and dominant energy pathways in the TC and ET phases are quantified in <a id="d7974185e5740" href="#fig16">Fig. 16</a>.
</p><div id="fig15" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f15.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 15."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-9555a3ae-7586-4a3a-a0ef-ff482ec67977" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
