<p>Temporal evolution of EKE tendency (<i>DK<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i>) and EAPE tendency (<i>DA<sub>E</sub></i>/<i>Dt</i>) for TC Hagibis (2019). Red, orange, green, and blue dots indicate the beginning of the cyclone life cycle as a tropical storm, ET onset, ET completion, and the end of the JMA best track record, respectively. The green line and the blue line indicate the TC and ET phases, respectively.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f15.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f15.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
Temporal evolution of EKE tendency (DKE/Dt) and EAPE tendency (DAE/Dt) for TC Hagibis (2019). Red, orange, green, and blue dots indicate the beginning of the cyclone life cycle as a tropical storm, ET onset, ET completion, and the end of the JMA best track record, respectively. The green line and the blue line indicate the TC and ET phases, respectively." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
Temporal evolution of EKE tendency (DKE/Dt) and EAPE tendency (DAE/Dt) for TC Hagibis (2019). Red, orange, green, and blue dots indicate the beginning of the cyclone life cycle as a tropical storm, ET onset, ET completion, and the end of the JMA best track record, respectively. The green line and the blue line indicate the TC and ET phases, respectively." data-header-text="Fig. 15." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f15.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div>
<div id="fig16" class="figure"><figure><div class="figure-body flex flex-col flex@md-row"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="figure-image-wrapper w-1 my-auto"><div style="max-width: 450px;" class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/inline-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f16.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" style="max-width: 450px;" alt="Fig. 16."/><div id="" role="" class="position-absolute display-none" data-popover="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-d6eb81d9-a466-43fb-a455-2fce4ea8e11b" data-popover-breakpoints="" data-popover-placement="" data-popover-fullscreen="true">
				
	        <div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: calc(100vw - 40px); height: calc(100vh - 40px);" class="c-Paper c-Paper--elevation-3 display-flex flex-col flex@xs-row pt-2 pr-2 pb-2 pl-2 overflow-scroll" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		<span class="right">
	        			<button id="basebutton_89" tabIndex="0" role="button" type="button" class=" c-IconButton c-IconButton--medium c-Icon c-Icon--close c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium ">

	
		
	

</button>
	        		</span>
	        		<div class="popover-body m-auto flex h-1"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f16.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 16."/></div></div></div>
	        	
	        	
	        </div>
	    
			</div><button class=" c-Button c-Button--medium c-Button--full c-Icon c-Icon--fullscreen c-Icon--before c-Icon--offset c-Icon--medium " type="button" role="button" tabIndex="0" id="basebutton_0_41" data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" data-popover-anchor="304ecca3-cc7a-4b67-844b-16f919f6459c-d6eb81d9-a466-43fb-a455-2fce4ea8e11b">View Full Size

	
		
	

</button><pf-box data-xsl-identifier="figure-popover" class="figure-popover display-none"><div class="display-table m-auto"><div class="image-wrapper flex flex-col flex-align-start"><img data-image-src="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f16.jpg" src="/skin/710ad8a6b2413221890b4b07720ada0d29d8e951/img/Blank.svg" class="lazy-load" alt="Fig. 16."/></div></div></pf-box></div></div><div class="figure-text-wrapper pl-3"><figcaption><span style="font-variant: small-caps;">Fig</span>. 16.</figcaption>
<p>As in <a id="d7974185e5775" href="#fig1">Fig. 1</a>, but for the (top) TC phase and (bottom) ET phase of TC Hagibis (2019). The value inside each box represents the tendency of local eddy energy (W m<sup>−2</sup>), while the arrows denote the energy budget terms (W m<sup>−2</sup>). Blue arrows indicate the main energy pathways, while thick blue arrows highlight the enhanced energy processes during the ET phase compared to the TC phase.</p><p class="citation">Citation: Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences 83, 9; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1">10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1</a></p><ul class="c-List border-dark m-0 mt-2"><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><a download="JAS-D-26-0015.1-f16.tif" href="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1-f16.tif">Download Figure</a></li><li class="download-figure mt-2 mb-2 pl-3 pr-3"><div class="ppt"><a doi="10.1175/JAS-D-26-0015.1" current-uri="/journals/atsc/83/9/JAS-D-26-0015.1.xml" alt-text="
As in Fig. 1, but for the (top) TC phase and (bottom) ET phase of TC Hagibis (2019). The value inside each box represents the tendency of local eddy energy (W m−2), while the arrows denote the energy budget terms (W m−2). Blue arrows indicate the main energy pathways, while thick blue arrows highlight the enhanced energy processes during the ET phase compared to the TC phase." attrib="" watermark-path="AMS-Logo-Lockup-Journals-01.png" data-footer-text="
As in Fig. 1, but for the (top) TC phase and (bottom) ET phase of TC Hagibis (2019). The value inside each box represents the tendency of local eddy energy (W m−2), while the arrows denote the energy budget terms (W m−2). Blue arrows indicate the main energy pathways, while thick blue arrows highlight the enhanced energy processes during the ET phase compared to the TC phase." data-header-text="Fig. 16." data-image-uris="/view/journals/atsc/83/9/full-JAS-D-26-0015.1-f16.jpg" href="#" class="export-figure-ppt">Download figure as PowerPoint slide</a></div></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><p></p>
<p>The major energy terms governing the evolution of EAPE and EKE not only intensify in magnitude but also become more asymmetric during the ET phase. The enhanced and asymmetric EAPE generation results from both diabatic heating associated with asymmetric precipitation under the jet–TC interaction and asymmetric temperature advection. The enhanced meridional eddy heat flux reduces the background temperature gradient, thus enhancing the APE conversion from the background flow to the eddy. Baroclinic energy conversion is enhanced ahead of the storm due to asymmetric temperature anomalies and strong upward motion related to jet–TC interaction. The eddy geopotential flux divergence intensifies along the boundary of the western North Pacific high and is further enhanced by upper-level divergent TC outflow, producing a stronger and more asymmetric EKE boundary flux. Overall, the increase in magnitude and spatial asymmetry of the energy terms during the ET phase largely results from interaction with the midlatitude baroclinic environment.</p>
<p>It should be noted that the energetics perspective on ET dynamics has also been explored in previous studies. Using EKE diagnostics, <a id="ref_bib36" href="#bib36">Keller et al. (2014)</a> examined why forecast scenarios diverge substantially during ET and how such differences connect to downstream midlatitude flow and storm reintensification. <a id="ref_bib35" href="#bib35">Jung and Lackmann (2021)</a> showed that, in quasi-idealized simulations, future ET involves greater EKE through intensified baroclinic conversion, leading to stronger downstream amplification. These studies suggest that ET can act as an energy source that triggers or modifies midlatitude baroclinic waves (<a id="d7974185e5794" href="#bib56">Riemer et al. 2008</a>; <a id="ref_bib54" href="#bib54">Riemer and Jones 2010</a>, <a id="ref_bib55" href="#bib55">2014</a>), a process closely linked to the concept of downstream development (<a id="d7974185e5804" href="#bib48">Orlanski and Katzfey 1991</a>; <a id="d7974185e5807" href="#bib49">Orlanski and Chang 1993</a>; <a id="d7974185e5810" href="#bib50">Orlanski and Sheldon 1995</a>). Previous studies have also focused on the energetics of the ET process itself. <a id="ref_bib63" href="#bib63">Sun et al. (2012)</a>, for instance, analyzed the KE budget during the ET of Typhoon Yagi (2006) using high-resolution numerical model output. More generally, the energetics framework has been applied to understand the behavior of synoptic- and mesoscale cyclones (e.g., <a id="ref_bib61" href="#bib61">Shimada et al. 2014</a>; <a id="ref_bib15" href="#bib15">De Souza et al. 2025</a>, <a id="ref_bib16" href="#bib16">2026</a>). Building on these studies, the present study provides a more comprehensive diagnosis of the energy cycle during ET. Energetics is evaluated within an effective domain of storm influence using a storm-following coordinate. This framework requires no quasigeostrophic assumptions, allowing the tropical and baroclinic midlatitude environments to be treated under the same diagnostics as the storm moves into the midlatitudes. Furthermore, by encompassing both EAPE and EKE throughout the ET process, this study addresses not only the dynamical evolution of the system but also the role of EAPE as an energy source.</p>
<p>Previous studies have often employed a PV framework to describe TC–midlatitude interactions (<a id="d7974185e5830" href="#bib5">Atallah and Bosart 2003</a>; <a id="d7974185e5833" href="#bib2">Agustí-Panareda et al. 2004</a>; <a id="ref_bib20" href="#bib20">Evans and Prater-Mayes 2004</a>; <a id="d7974185e5839" href="#bib6">Atallah et al. 2007</a>; <a id="d7974185e5842" href="#bib27">Grams et al. 2013a</a>). <a id="d7974185e5846" href="#bib2">Agustí-Panareda et al. (2004)</a> proposed a sequence of PV-based stages describing the transition of Hurricane Irene (1999). Similar evolution was also found during the transition of Typhoon Jangmi (2008) (<a id="d7974185e5849" href="#bib27">Grams et al. 2013a</a>). The sequence begins as the TC approaches a midlatitude baroclinic zone, where vertical wind shear tilts the PV tower. Depending on the intensity and size of the TC, this shear-induced PV asymmetry may be axisymmetrized, but it can also promote convection on the outer flank of the storm (<a id="ref_bib14" href="#bib14">Davis et al. 2008</a>). The new convective burst appears as a narrow PV tower and induces intense deep ascent, which transitions into isentropic outflow in the upper troposphere. This outflow accelerates near the jet core under a steep vertical potential temperature gradient, intensifying the jet streak. Symmetric instability further contributes to this jet acceleration by enabling moist, low-PV air to undergo slantwise ascent and enhancing the isentropic flow toward the jet core.</p>
<p>The local energetics framework does not directly match the PV framework but offers complementary perspectives on TC–midlatitude interaction (<a id="ref_bib64" href="#bib64">Teubler and Riemer 2016</a>; <a id="ref_bib65" href="#bib65">Wirth et al. 2018</a>; <a id="d7974185e5864" href="#bib37">Keller et al. 2019</a>). Unlike in the PV framework where diabatic heating appears explicitly as diabatic PV production, diabatic heating affects multiple terms in the local energetics framework. Diabatic heating contributes directly to the EAPE generation, modifies the temperature anomalies involved in the APE conversion between background and eddy, and influences the baroclinic energy conversion through diabatically induced ascent. Combining the two frameworks would allow for a more rigorous and physically comprehensive interpretation of the interaction between the TC and the midlatitude baroclinic environment.</p>
<p>The local energetics framework can be used to interpret the direct and indirect impacts of ET in an energetics perspective. The wind field expansion and associated wind extremes during ET have been attributed to the inward transport of absolute angular momentum and to cooling near the storm center, which together cause the radius of maximum wind to shift outward (<a id="d7974185e5870" href="#bib17">Evans and Hart 2008</a>). However, these processes have not been examined through the lens of an energy cycle. Applying the local energetics framework to wind field expansion during ET may help identify which energy terms are most critical in driving the intensification, asymmetry, and outward expansion of high-wind regions. Moreover, evaluating how numerical models represent these key energetic processes would offer a means to diagnose why some models struggle to reproduce wind extremes during ET.</p>
<p>This study has a clear limitation in that the local energetics framework is applied to only a single ET case. We are currently applying this framework to multiple ET cases in the western North Pacific. This will help evaluate whether the intensified and asymmetric energy fluxes during ET are a consistent feature of TC interaction with the midlatitude baroclinic environment. Another limitation is the presence of nonnegligible residuals in the energy budgets. We expect that composite analyses will help reduce the residuals.</p></div>

<ack xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:ifp="http://www.ifactory.com/press">
